WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:03.821
Back to the move.

2
00:00:03.821 --> 00:00:07.917
Our helper prints a builder for every box with an @Builder note.

3
00:00:07.917 --> 00:00:12.780
Now someone puts the note on an interface, Wardrobe, and Kotlin allows that.

4
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:15.632
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

5
00:00:15.632 --> 00:00:20.442
When a processor meets something it can't handle, why not just throw an exception?

6
00:00:20.442 --> 00:00:22.788
That's how the rest of our code says no.

7
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:26.804
Lesson 4's processor does just that, by accident.

8
00:00:26.804 --> 00:00:31.990
It reads the primary constructor with two exclamation marks: I'm sure it's there.

9
00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.399
An interface has none.

10
00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:35.448
So what will the build tell you?

11
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:38.865
This.

12
00:00:38.865 --> 00:00:42.529
A NullPointerException, with no error message.

13
00:00:42.529 --> 00:00:45.079
No file, no line, no class name.

14
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:50.847
Ask for the stack trace, the list of calls that were running when it was thrown.

15
00:00:50.847 --> 00:00:57.614
Over a hundred lines down sits the cause: constructorParameters, in BuilderProcessor.kt, line 88.

16
00:00:57.614 --> 00:01:00.265
Every frame points into the processor.

17
00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:03.683
None points at Wardrobe.kt, where the mistake is.

18
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.834
It's a very detailed tour of the helper's own pockets.

19
00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:13.104
So the point is, a crash shows where the processor broke, not where the user went wrong.

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.330
And a crash does more harm than a bad message.

21
00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:28.404
It stops KSP on the spot: the rest of the round never runs, nor do the other processors, and in KSP 2.3.14 even onError, the clean-up method, is skipped.

22
00:01:28.404 --> 00:01:32.964
The helper dropped the clipboard in the hallway and walked off.

23
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:40.892
What we want is the opposite: a helper who sticks a note on the wrong box itself, saying what's wrong and what to do.

24
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.042
Let's map it.

25
00:01:42.042 --> 00:01:47.669
The note is a message from KSPLogger, the logger KSP hands every processor.

26
00:01:47.669 --> 00:01:51.721
The box is a symbol: KSP's object for one declaration.

27
00:01:51.721 --> 00:01:55.022
Its place in the house is the file and line.

28
00:01:55.022 --> 00:01:56.823
And the words are yours.

29
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.735
The logger has error, warn, info and logging, from loudest to quietest.

30
00:02:01.735 --> 00:02:06.336
Java's annotation processors have the same tool, called the Messager.

31
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:08.949
Now let's climb a ladder.

32
00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:17.498
Three bad boxes stay the same all the way: Wardrobe, an interface; Settings, an object; and Crate, with no primary constructor.

33
00:02:17.498 --> 00:02:19.383
Each rung changes one thing.

34
00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:23.721
Rung one, no check at all: the NullPointerException we just saw.

35
00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:28.844
Rung two adds a check, and calls logger.error with a message but no symbol.

36
00:02:28.844 --> 00:02:33.025
The build fails with a sentence now: @Builder can't build this.

37
00:02:33.025 --> 00:02:34.020
But which this?

38
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.125
It's like a note on the fridge that says: somebody broke something.

39
00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:40.645
True, and you still search the whole house.

40
00:02:40.645 --> 00:02:42.988
A message without a symbol has no where.

41
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:46.691
Rung three passes the symbol as the second argument.

42
00:02:46.691 --> 00:02:48.949
What does KSP add to the message?

43
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.740
The where: Crate.kt, colon 6, in front of the message.

44
00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:03.149
A real Gradle build prints the full path, which a good editor turns into a link, and a line, but no column.

45
00:03:03.333 --> 00:03:08.060
But it reported Crate and stopped, and Wardrobe and Settings were just as wrong.

46
00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.368
The early stop was our choice: a return inside the loop.

47
00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:24.797
Swap it for continue, the next section's fix, and the top rung puts a note on every bad box in one build: Wardrobe.kt line 6, Wardrobe.kt line 9, Crate.kt line 6.

48
00:03:24.797 --> 00:03:28.891
One walk, then one phone call, not a call per box.

49
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.306
Now a detail that trips up almost everyone.

50
00:03:33.306 --> 00:03:39.373
In Wardrobe.kt, the @Builder note is on line 5, and interface Wardrobe is on line 6.

51
00:03:39.373 --> 00:03:41.612
Which line does the error name?

52
00:03:44.867 --> 00:03:45.363
Line 6.

53
00:03:45.363 --> 00:03:50.180
A symbol's location is its declaration's line, not its annotation's.

54
00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.289
The helper's note goes on the box, not on the sticky note.

55
00:03:54.289 --> 00:03:56.840
So a test that expects line 5 fails.

56
00:03:57.733 --> 00:04:03.663
The symbol says where, so your sentence has two jobs: what is wrong, and how to fix it.

57
00:04:03.663 --> 00:04:13.000
Read it as a user who never saw the processor's code: the annotation, the class, its kind, the next step, and no word from KSP's own API.

58
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.102
So every error names its symbol.

59
00:04:15.102 --> 00:04:18.845
The symbol is the where; your sentence is the what and the how.

60
00:04:19.933 --> 00:04:22.680
In both languages, the symbol goes along.

61
00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.360
Kotlin passes the class to logger.error.

62
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:35.143
Java passes the element to the Messager's printMessage, with the kind ERROR, and javac prints the file and line, with the source line and a caret.

63
00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.319
Two guardrails.

64
00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:41.578
First, a trap with a polite name: logger.exception.

65
00:04:41.578 --> 00:04:48.844
It prints a line that starts with e, like an error, but in KSP 2.3.14 the build passes.

66
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.730
It should have been called logger.mention.

67
00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:54.069
If you mean fail, call logger.error.

68
00:04:54.267 --> 00:04:56.163
Second: check before you read.

69
00:04:56.163 --> 00:05:04.947
Every two exclamation marks, and every as cast on something from the Resolver, is a place where a check belongs, with a note on the symbol.

70
00:05:05.867 --> 00:05:07.356
So, why not just throw?

71
00:05:07.356 --> 00:05:12.085
An exception points into the processor, stops everyone, and names no box.

72
00:05:12.085 --> 00:05:16.490
logger.error with the symbol points at the user's own file and line.

73
00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:20.571
The symbol is the where; your sentence is the what and the how.

74
00:05:20.733 --> 00:05:21.580
Quick check.

75
00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:26.801
In the same file, @Builder is on line 8, and object Settings is on line 9.

76
00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:28.988
Which line does the error name?

77
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.716
Line 9, the declaration's line.

78
00:05:34.716 --> 00:05:40.235
That's the real output: Wardrobe.kt, colon 9, Settings is an object.
